Subject: [PATCH 2/6] moe: a parameter accepted in compose()/emit() and then dropped at the call

Not four bugs — one bug class, four instances, in a single op family. The shape
is always the same: a parameter is threaded through the public surface, has a
default that is correct for the model the op was written against, and is then
either ignored or replaced by a literal at the inner call. Nothing fails. The
model runs with the default.

INSTANCES (all `blaze/ops/`, line numbers at HEAD)

1. `glm_moe_router/op.py:137` — `num_total_experts=256` as a literal, while
   `num_experts` is accepted at `:94`, read from user args at `:63` and passed
   down at `:71`. The parameter arrives and is discarded one line short of use.
   Consequence: a 64-expert model ranks 256 gate lanes over 64 written ones.
   Fixed by patch 1 of 6.
2. `glm_moe_router/op.py:138` — `pop_act=True` as a literal at the gate call,
   while `pop_act` is a real parameter used for the gate MM at `:103`. Same name,
   two meanings, one of them a constant. Compounding this, the gate's `pop_act`
   is INERT: `generic_moe_gate/kernels/op.hpp:47` is its only occurrence in the
   kernel, which pops act and bias unconditionally at lines 92-93. Fixed by
   patch 1 of 6 (call site) and documented in `GenericMoeGate.emit`.
3. `glm_moe/op.py` — never accepted `num_experts` at all, in `compose()` or
   `emit()`, so no caller could set it and `GLMRoutedExpert`'s 256 default always
   won. Fixed here.
4. `glm_moe_no_shared/op.py` — accepted `num_experts` in BOTH `compose()` and
   `emit()`, then omitted it from the `GLMRoutedExpert.emit` call. The most
   legible instance of the class: the plumbing is complete except for the last
   hop. Fixed here.

`glm_routed_expert/op.py` is the intermediate hop and never had the parameter
either; added here so 3 and 4 have somewhere to send it.

THREE FURTHER SITES, SAME DEFECT, DIFFERENT OUTCOME

`gptoss_moe_router/op.py:162`, `ffn_interleaved/op.py:476` and
`deepseekv4_moe_large_router/op.py:156` pass `num_total_experts=num_experts` —
the raw model count, unrounded. These are not silent: below 128, or at any count
that is not a multiple of 128, the kernel's static_assert
(`ckernel_sfpu_generic_moe_gate_topk.h:176-177`) fails the JIT build. They are
included here because they make the point. The same mistake is safe when it
trips a bound and unsafe when it hits a default, and which one you get is
incidental. All three also pass the inert `pop_act=True`
(`:163`, `:477`, `:157`). Patch 1 of 6 turns their failure mode into a Python
`ValueError` that names the helper to call; none of the three is otherwise
changed, since we have no numerics gate for those models — NOT RUN.

WHAT WOULD HAVE CAUGHT IT

Nothing in the existing suite, which is the point worth acting on. Every device
test for this family runs 128 or 256 experts, where the wrong value equals the
right one. `tests/blaze/micro_ops/moe/test_moe_router_gate_wiring.py`, added
here, parses the router and MoE sources with `ast` and asserts the wiring
directly: that `num_total_experts` is derived from a plan rather than a literal,
that `num_experts` reaches every inner call that accepts it, and that the inert
`pop_act` is not passed. It is CPU-only (`pytest.mark.device("cpu")`), needs no
device, and would have failed on all four instances above.

ALSO IN THIS PATCH, AND NOT FIXED

`glm_moe/op.py:170` derives the routed-expert output lane from mesh position:
`index_offset = (row * cols + col) // routed_expert_tp + routed_index_offset`.
That ranges over `[0, num_devices/tp)` — 0..31 on a 4x8 mesh — while the gate
only fills lanes `[0, num_selected_experts)` and `zero_tail=True` blanks the
rest. Beyond k, a device reads expert id 0 with score 0: it recomputes expert 0
and multiplies the result by zero. At GLM-4.7-Flash's k=4 on 32 devices that is
28 of 32 devices doing silent no-op work; VERIFIED by reading the derivation and
the `zero_tail` semantics, not measured. The condition for validity is
`num_devices / routed_expert_tp <= k`. We deliberately do NOT patch it: the
correct behaviour is an expert-parallel mapping decision (k devices per expert
group with the group id folded in, or a lane->device assignment the router emits)
and not something this op can decide locally. Both `glm_moe.emit` and
`GLMRoutedExpert.emit` gain comments marking it UNRESOLVED so the next reader
does not have to rediscover it.

STATUS
* `num_experts` threading VERIFIED on silicon as part of the GLM-4.7-Flash
  `glm_routed_expert` run that patch 1 of 6 fixed; without it the router keeps
  ranking 256 lanes regardless of patch 1.
* Wiring test CPU-only, NOT RUN ON HARDWARE, needs no device.

Apply after patch 1 of 6.
